ng with the bottom  going from the going from inside of the  cell and interacting with the membrane  then from the outside of the cell  and  sure they don't have any charge and so  they're not going to be particularly  attracted to the hydrophilic head of our  phospholipids but they're also not going  to be repelled by them and they're going  to have more on the inside interacting  with the membrane than the outside and  so since they're small some of them are  going to be able to pass through and  they're also not going to be bothered by  the hydrophobic tails and they're going  to have things going both ways but  you're going to have more going from the  inside to the outside than from the  outside to the inside so they're going  to move along with their concentration  gradient so carbon dioxide can actually  diffuse quite well across cellular  membranes another molecule that can is  molecular oxygen  molecular oxygen can also diffuse quite  well across cellular membranes so if i  have a higher concentration of out of  oxygen on the outside than i have on the  inside because it's small and it's  non-charged it's not going to have  problems you know it's not going to be  particularly attracted to the  hydrophilic heads but they're small  they're going to be able to pass right  between them it's going to be  indifferent to them and then it's going  to be able to pass through all these  hydrophobic tails and since you have a  higher concentration on the outside than  the inside you're just going to have  more in a given amount of time more  random interactions of the ones going in  that direction than the ones going in  that direction so you would have a net  inflow into the cells so these things  are going to be able to diffuse fairly  fairly whoops these things are going to  be able to diffuse fairly fairly  naturally now and of course they are  going to be obstructed by by just the  structure by all of these molecules here  that make up the actual cellular  membra